"\n\n<h3>I. Included, Then Pushed Back</h3>\n\n<p>The previous essay ended with a promise newly claimed, one that would be dismantled piece by piece over the following decades. The years from Grant's inauguration in 1869 to Hayes's final year in office trace precisely that arc: how the deepest remainder was brought into the center at last, and how it was then watched as it was pressed back to the margins.</p>\n\n<p>The best way to grasp the structure of this period is not to choose between success and failure, but to hold both as simultaneously true. First, the Fifteenth Amendment inscribed Black male suffrage into the Constitution, and the South saw something genuinely unprecedented — Black voters, Black state legislators, Black local officials, Black members of Congress participating in American political life for the first time at scale. Second, this inclusion was swiftly met by organized white violence, by the so-called Redemption of Southern state governments, by the Supreme Court's constriction of federal power, and finally by a political bargain and a troop withdrawal that abandoned what had been won.</p>\n\n<p>The key distinction to hold from the outset: being pushed back is not the same as being erased. The Fifteenth Amendment was not repealed. Its text remained in the Constitution, the promise of equality persisting as a latent constitutional obligation even as its enforcement was hollowed out. This is the deep paradox this essay tracks — the construct had acknowledged the debt at the highest level but lacked the capacity or will to repay it on the ground. The remainder was left suspended: neither redeemed nor eliminated, lodged in a gap between the written word and the lived fact.</p>\n\n<h3>II. Grant, a Paradoxical Figure</h3>\n\n<p>Understanding this period requires understanding Grant as a paradox. He won in 1868 primarily on the strength of his Civil War reputation, and on the support of nearly half a million Black voters across the South. He brought almost no prior political experience to office — he later admitted as much himself. Yet the modern historiography of Reconstruction has increasingly rehabilitated his record on the core civil rights question.</p>\n\n<p>Grant genuinely feared that Klan violence was reversing the verdict of the war. He supported and signed the Enforcement Acts, deployed federal troops and the Justice Department against the Klan, and saw himself as a defender of the rights newly inscribed in the Constitution. The corruption scandals that marred his presidency — Crédit Mobilier, the Whiskey Ring — are real, but modern scholarship is careful to note that Grant himself was never a target of investigation and did not profit personally. His failing was loyalty to the wrong men, not personal venality.</p>\n\n<p>What Grant's presidency represents in this series is something worth marking precisely. For the first time, executive power was deployed on a large scale to protect the remainder that had just been included — not to wage war, not to expand territory, but to shield the people newly written into the Constitution's guarantees. The Enforcement Acts and the prosecutions that followed briefly worked. They disrupted the Klan, and the election of 1872 was relatively fair. This matters enormously for the anti-fatalist reading of Reconstruction's collapse: the failure was not inevitable.</p>\n\n<h3>III. The Fifteenth Amendment: Constitutional Zenith of Inclusion</h3>\n\n<p>The Fifteenth Amendment was the high-water mark of Reconstruction's inclusion. It prohibited denying or abridging the right to vote \"on account of race, color, or previous condition of servitude,\" and empowered Congress to enforce it by appropriate legislation. Grant called it, in 1870, a measure of greater importance than any act since the founding.</p>\n\n<p>The political changes it enabled were real. By 1870, Republicans controlled Southern state governments; Black men served in state legislatures and in local offices; between 1870 and 1901, twenty-two Black men served in Congress. Hiram Revels entered the Senate in February 1870; Joseph Rainey became the first popularly elected Black member of the House that December. Reconstruction state governments built the South's first publicly funded school systems — South Carolina's enrollment rose from roughly thirty thousand to one hundred twenty-three thousand between 1868 and 1876.</p>\n\n<p>But the amendment's limits were built into its text from the start. It forbade denial of the vote on racial grounds — not all voter qualifications. This left the precise gap that Southern states would later exploit: poll taxes, literacy tests, grandfather clauses, and white primaries all operated without ever naming race, yet achieved racial exclusion with surgical precision. The inclusion at the constitutional summit contained the mechanism of its own circumvention. Paper inclusion and ground-level exclusion could coexist indefinitely, and they would.</p>\n\n<h3>IV. Reconstruction Governments and the Corruption Narrative</h3>\n\n<p>The long-running story of corrupt and incompetent Reconstruction governments requires revision — and that revision is itself part of this essay's argument. Modern historians, following the lead of W. E. B. Du Bois and later Eric Foner, have not denied that corruption existed. What they have denied is that corruption can be taken as the essential truth of Reconstruction. The graft that did occur was mostly perpetrated by white officials and merchants, not by Black voters or legislators; and the corruption of Reconstruction-era Southern governments was no worse than, and often milder than, the corruption then flourishing in Northern politics, the railroads, and the Gilded Age machine bosses.</p>\n\n<p>The corruption narrative is structural, not merely incidental, to how the remainder was pushed back. It served as ideological cover for disfranchisement. If Black political participation was inherently corrupt and incompetent, then removing Black voters from politics could be framed as reform rather than racial oppression — a correction of democratic malfunctioning rather than a violation of democratic rights. Dismantling Reconstruction was packaged as cleaning house. Seeing through that packaging is a precondition for understanding what was actually happening.</p>\n\n<h3>V. White Violence and Federal Legal Counter-Measures</h3>\n\n<p>The white Southern counter-movement must be written as organized political terror, not as social unrest. The Klan and its affiliates were not mobs out of control. They were pursuing a political program: beat, rape, murder, and burn enough Black citizens and white Republican officeholders that Black civil rights and Republican state governments became impossible to sustain. The Memphis massacre of May 1866 killed forty-six Black residents; the New Orleans massacre of July 1866 killed thirty-four within two hours. Grant's most significant test came with the Colfax massacre of April 1873, in which at least sixty-two Black men — and by some estimates closer to one hundred fifty — were killed in a dispute over control of a Louisiana courthouse.</p>\n\n<p>Grant's response was the Enforcement Acts of 1870–71. The third, the Ku Klux Klan Act, authorized the president to suspend habeas corpus and deploy the military. Grant used it. In October 1871 he declared a state of emergency in nine South Carolina counties, suspended habeas corpus, and sent federal forces to arrest suspects. By year's end more than six hundred people had been detained; dozens were convicted. The Klan was disrupted. The 1872 election was relatively fair. The episode proves that the inclusion was defensible — if the construct chose to keep defending it.</p>\n\n<h3>VI. Judicial Blunting, Economic Crisis, the Decay of Will</h3>\n\n<p>Why did the protection not continue? The answer is not a single cause but a convergence of several, and they must be held together.</p>\n\n<p>The Supreme Court was the first. In United States v. Cruikshank, arising directly from the Colfax massacre, the Court held that the Fourteenth Amendment's equal protection clause reached only state action, not private violence. The murders committed at Colfax were returned to Southern state courts — which meant, in practice, to the very system that had no intention of prosecuting them. The legal weapon forged to protect the newly included was blunted by the institution charged with interpreting the Constitution.</p>\n\n<p>The economic crisis of 1873 — triggered by railroad speculation, the contraction of European credit, and a cascade of bank failures — lasted more than five years. Northern attention turned inward; Republican anxiety about Southern intervention and its political costs grew; the moral energy that had survived the war began to dissipate. And underlying all of this was something more structural: most white Northerners did not, in fact, genuinely accept Black civil and political equality. As Foner has noted, the failure to address the economic demands of the freedpeople — land above all — meant that formal political rights were chronically exposed to coercion.</p>\n\n<p>These forces combined to enable Redemption: the systematic overthrow of Republican state governments by Southern Democrats. The construct had demonstrated with Grant's Enforcement Acts that it could protect what it had promised, but it required sustained national capacity — troops, willing prosecutors, fair courts, continuing political will — that could not survive the economic shock and the moral exhaustion of the 1870s.</p>\n\n<h3>VII. The 1876 Election and the Compromise of 1877</h3>\n\n<p>The disputed election of 1876 was the last moment at which Reconstruction's political defenses were openly negotiated at the national level. Tilden led in the popular vote, but three Southern states returned conflicting electoral certificates. A special Electoral Commission awarded all disputed votes to Hayes, giving him a one-vote margin. The political settlement that followed — informal rather than a written treaty — saw the Hayes administration adopt a policy of \"local self-government,\" withdraw federal troops from Southern statehouses, and effectively end active federal protection of Black Republican governments in the South. Hayes removed federal soldiers from South Carolina and Louisiana in April 1877.</p>\n\n<p>Two readings of this settlement exist and both should be recorded. The realist reading: Hayes inherited a situation in which almost no federal troops remained in the South, the Democratic House refused to fund further deployments, and Northern opinion had turned. Military Reconstruction could not have continued much longer regardless. The moral reading: whatever the constraints, the withdrawal exposed Black Southerners to systematic violence, judicial indifference, and disfranchisement that would define the next eighty years. Hayes himself later complained of the fraud, intimidation, and violence used against Black voters in 1878 — but by then the troops were gone.</p>\n\n<p>The deeper irony is worth noting. Earlier essays in this series identified the construct's openness — its retained electoral mechanism — as its tool of self-correction. Lincoln used it to sustain legitimacy through the Civil War; Congress used the 1866 midterms to reclaim the terms of Reconstruction from Andrew Johnson. By 1877, the same openness became the vehicle for abandoning the promise. The mechanism is not on the side of the remainder automatically. It only provides the arena. What plays out on that stage depends on whether those with votes and power choose to treat the promise as binding.</p>\n\n<h3>VIII. Conclusion</h3>\n\n<p>The twelve years from Grant to Hayes are most accurately described by the twin judgment: included, then pushed back. Both are real, neither is metaphor. The inclusion happened — a constitutional amendment, mass Black participation in political life, Black men in Congress and in office across the South. The pushing back happened — through terror, judicial narrowing, economic crisis, the evaporation of Northern will, and a backroom settlement that swapped troop protection for a presidential outcome.</p>\n\n<p>But the pushing back was not a closure. The Fifteenth Amendment remained. The promise of equality survived in the Constitution as a latent obligation, waiting — for decades — to be reactivated. The old order hollowed out the enforcement but could not erase the text. That gap between the written commitment and the lived reality is not a sign of successful closure; it is the signature of the irreducibly open. The remainder was neither redeemed nor destroyed. It was suspended.</p>\n\n<p>Reconstruction's failure was not fated. Grant's Enforcement Acts proved the inclusion was defensible when the construct chose to defend it. The collapse resulted from contingent factors that could have been otherwise — judicial retreat, economic shock, decaying will, the gap never bridged between recognizing the debt and actually paying it. To blame a single person, or to call the outcome inevitable, is to miss the truth: it was a dismantling carried out by many hands, none of which had to do what it did.</p>\n\n      "
